            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for technique, then the Mandarin for to make progress."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: jīngyàn",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "jīngyàn — \"experience; know-how\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tā hěn yǒu jīngyàn — He's very experienced."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "jīngyàn is built from jīng and yàn, both inherited from Middle Chinese, keng and ngjemH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'constant' and 'the warp of cloth', and the second 'to verify'. How the pair came to mean experience is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for talking about the past."
